        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="true" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="true" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="3">
                    <English>No Vermiculite</English>
                    <French>Pas de Vermiculite</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <HouseholdOperatingConditions numberOfOccupants="4" coolingSeasonMonths="0" isClothesWasherEnergySaver="false" isLightingEnergySaver="true" areAppliancesEnergySaver="false">
                <Thermostat heatingDaytime="21" heatingNighttime="18" cooling="25" />
            </HouseholdOperatingConditions>
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the two doors, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). 



Air leakage was also observed through both bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model. If you will be considering adding an heat recovery ventilator (please see ventilation section of this report), these bathroom exhaust fans may be eliminated altogether, as the HRV could be tied into the two bathrooms. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 50% improvement (bringing the air changes per hour to 3.7)  you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,500&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;37.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 700 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x4 studs with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2 inches of expanded polystyrene. I have modelled adding an additional 2 inches (R8) of expanded polystyrene, and replacing the fiberglass batt with roxul (R14), for a total nominal insulation value of R30.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding an additional 2 inches (R8) of expanded polystyrene, and replacing the fiberglass with roxul (R14), you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 450&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of the entrance room was assumed to have 4 inches (R12) fiberglass batt insulation, as was found in the original main section of the home (below the cathedral addition). I have modelled bringing this insulation value up to R40.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By bringing this insulation value to R40, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 21 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; (as the rebate is pro-rated based on area) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house appears to have 9.5 inches (R31) fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to the cathedral ceiling, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 240&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</ceilingsCathedral>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 4 inches of expanded polystyrene insulation. I have modeled adding a 2x4 framed wall with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation, for a total nominal insualtion value of R28. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 420&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 625 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be sufficient ventilation for your home. However, as you will be conducting quite a bit of air sealing and re-wrapping the exterior walls of the home, the current ventilation may no longer be enough. 

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STARÂ® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

As you are getting into the wall cavities with this retrofit, these exhaust ports could be installed into the kitchen and bathroom, potentially eliminating the need of using exhaust only fans (such as the bathroom fan which is currently installed)Â while the fresh air supply into the other rooms could use the duct work that is already in place for the furnace, minimizing the installation requirements. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;87% AFUE Mid-Efficiency&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE, however a recent combustion test would indicate the current efficiency. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified mid-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. In this configuration, the oil burner on the furnace requires an outside combustion air.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a mid-efficiency oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 710&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;18.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. An AFUE of 92% or higher is required to qualify for a rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;92% AFUE Condensing Oil Furnace&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



As a second option to consider, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 92% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 92% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;31.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hot Water Heater&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. As electricity is generated from diesel generators in your community, the overall efficiency is very low - diesel is used to produce electricity that is then used to produce your hot water. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an oil-fired tank water heater with an energy factor of 0.60 in a fuel community, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. There are currently no rebates available for installing an oil-fired tank water heater from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Combustion Air&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



I did see a combustion air supply pipe to provide combustion air to the furnace, however it was plugged with insulation.

I suggest you have a qualified heating contractor investigate this further and ensure both the fresh air supply requirements of your house and the combustion air supply needs of your furnace. The combustion air pipe should have an electric damper installed to ensure combustion air will only be introduced into the room when the furnace is operating. This will also apply if you are considering a fuel fired hot water heater that is not direct vented. 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Good Hope&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Good Hope:

Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh, $0.72 per kWh after Subsity Limit

Oil  - $1.44 per litre 

Wood - $210 per cord 



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



